y":-0.2362,"pass_rate":0.5,"pass_rate_neutral":0.3333,"def_epa_per_play":0.04128},{"week":16,"opponent":"MIA","home":false,"pf":35.0,"pa":38.0,"off_snaps":87,"epa_per_play":0.0558,"pass_rate":0.7011,"pass_rate_neutral":0.5,"def_epa_per_play":0.1795},{"week":17,"opponent":"CLE","home":true,"pf":33.0,"pa":23.0,"off_snaps":65,"epa_per_play":0.1434,"pass_rate":0.4462,"pass_rate_neutral":0.4909,"def_epa_per_play":0.02351}],"comps":{"offense":[{"season":2022,"team":"NE","distance":0.448,"shared":18,"play_caller":null},{"season":2020,"team":"DAL","distance":0.4837,"shared":18,"play_caller":"Kellen Moore"},{"season":2023,"team":"TB","distance":0.4904,"shared":15,"play_caller":"Dave Canales"},{"season":2024,"team":"NYJ","distance":0.5011,"shared":15,"play_caller":null},{"season":2020,"team":"DET","distance":0.5047,"shared":18,"play_caller":null}],"defense":[{"season":2022,"team":"CLE","distance":0.5935,"shared":18,"play_caller":null},{"season":2019,"team":"PHI","distance":0.6151,"shared":18,"play_caller":null},{"season":2023,"team":"WAS","distance":0.6382,"shared":18,"play_caller":null},{"season":2020,"team":"PHI","distance":0.6918,"shared":18,"play_caller":null},{"season":2023,"team":"CLE","distance":0.6971,"shared":18,"play_caller":null}]},"overview":{"overview":"The Bengals went 2-14 in 2019 and were outscored 420 to 279. Neither side held up, as the offense ranked 29th in EPA per play and the defense 31st in EPA allowed. What set them apart was the highest 11 personnel usage and the fewest linebackers on the field in the league.","offense":"The Bengals offense struggled, ranking 25th or worse in four of the five main efficiency measures. Their run and pass mix stayed close to what game situations called for. Schematically, they stood out for the highest 11 personnel usage and the 3rd lowest I formation usage in the league. Andy Dalton ran the passing game and leaned heavily on Tyler Boyd, who drew 25% of the targets.","defense":"The Bengals had one of the worst defenses in the league, ranking 25th or worse in all five main efficiency measures. Its strongest area was pressure rate, where it ranked 12th, and its weakest was explosive play rate allowed, where it ranked 32nd. Geno Atkins was as productive as expected, but the bigger surprise was Carl Lawson, who had 5 sacks despite playing only 57% of the snaps. In terms of scheme, they had the fewest linebackers on the field in the league.","special":"The Bengals were about average in how often they went for it on fourth down. Their kickers made 87% of field goals, which was well above average. Their salary cap was spread across positions in a fairly typical way. The offense also brought back more of its roster than most, with 88% of its snaps going to players who were on the team the year before.","players":"Andy Dalton played nearly every snap at quarterback and threw for 3,494 yards. Tyler Boyd was the clear top target with 25% of the looks and 1,046 receiving yards. Joe Mixon handled most of the work in the backfield with 81% of the carries. On defense, Carlos Dunlap led the team with 9 sacks and Darius Phillips picked off 4 passes.","coaching":"Zac Taylor called the offense as head coach and Lou Anarumo called the defense. Their offense looked most like the 2022 Patriots and the 2020 Cowboys under Kellen Moore. Their defense looked most like the 2022 Browns and the 2019 Eagles.","trends":"The offense went from 19th in EPA per play in 2018 to 29th in 2019, a big step back. The defense moved from 31st in EPA allowed per play in 2018 to 31st in 2019, about where it was. The biggest change in approach was number of defenders in the box, which went from 6.26 to 6.58.","notes":"FTN did not start charting plays until 2022, so play action, motion, RPO and screen rates are not available for 2019. Everything else, including personnel, formations and coverage, is complete for this season."}}